# GoPro Studio

A small terminal app for controlling a rig of GoPros over USB from Linux (or macOS/Windows):
connect, push identical settings, start/stop recording in sync, and pull the footage into
per-camera session folders — ready to hand to FreeMoCap's **SkellySync** for frame-accurate
alignment and 3D reconstruction.

It does **not** try to replace SkellySync's alignment — GoPros have no hardware genlock, so this
app focuses on getting all cameras rolling as close together as command-level sync allows
(typically well under a frame of skew over USB), and lets you clap or flash at the start of each
take so SkellySync's audio-cross-correlation / brightness-flash detection can nail exact
frame alignment on import.

Built on GoPro's official [`open-gopro`](https://gopro.github.io/OpenGoPro/python_sdk/) Python
SDK, using its `WiredGoPro` (USB HTTP) transport — no BLE/WiFi contention with 6 cameras.

## How it works

Each GoPro Hero9+ enumerates over USB as its own network interface (RNDIS/CDC-ECM) with its own
private subnet, so a powered USB hub can host several cameras at once, each independently
addressable over HTTP. The app talks to all of them concurrently with asyncio:

- **Connect**: opens a `WiredGoPro` session to every configured camera in parallel.
- **Push settings**: loads the Video preset and sets identical resolution/fps on every camera
  (consistent settings across views matters more for triangulation quality than shaving a
  few extra ms off sync).
- **Synced start/stop**: every camera is "armed" first; a shared barrier then releases all of
  them in the same event-loop tick, so the shutter command goes out to all 6 as close together
  as the USB stack allows — the same pattern GoPro's own official multi-camera demo uses.
- **Download**: pulls new files off each camera's SD card into `<output_dir>/<session>/<label>/`.

## Install (Linux)

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e .
```

Requires Python 3.11–3.13.

## Set up your rig

Plug in all 6 cameras via the powered USB hub, power them on, then either scan for them:

```bash
gopro-studio-setup discover
```

or add them by hand (the serial is the last 3-4 digits shown under the camera's
Settings > About screen, which also matches the tail of its default WiFi SSID):

```bash
gopro-studio-setup add 5124 cam1_front
gopro-studio-setup add 8871 cam2_left
# ...etc for all 6
gopro-studio-setup output ~/GoProStudio/sessions
gopro-studio-setup list
```

This writes `~/.config/gopro_studio/config.json`. Default settings are 4K/60 — edit
`resolution`/`fps` in that file to any of the `VideoResolution` / `FramesPerSecond` values from
`gopro_studio/rig.py` if you want something else (e.g. `NUM_5_3K` / `NUM_30_0`).

## Run it

```bash
gopro-studio
```

Keyboard shortcuts (also available as buttons): `c` connect all, `s` push settings,
`r` start recording (synced), `x` stop recording (synced), `d` download session,
`q` quit. Set the session name in the text field before downloading — files land in
`<output_dir>/<session_name>/<camera_label>/`.

Typical take:
1. `c` to connect, `s` to push settings once at the start of a shoot.
2. `r` to start — the moment recording confirms on all cameras, clap or flash.
3. Perform the capture.
4. `x` to stop.
5. Repeat `r`/`x` for more takes; when done, `d` to download everything.

## Linux networking notes

- The kernel's `rndis_host`/`cdc_ether` drivers handle GoPro's USB network interfaces
  automatically on virtually all modern distros — no special udev rules needed.
- NetworkManager should auto-configure each camera's interface. If `gopro-studio-setup discover`
  finds nothing but `lsusb` shows all 6 cameras, check `ip addr` for `usbX`/`enxX` interfaces
  without an IP, and that NetworkManager isn't set to ignore USB interfaces
  (`nmcli device status`).
- A **quality powered USB hub matters** — GoPro engineers have noted that at large camera counts,
  powered hubs "tend to enumerate flakily," recommending PCIe USB cards instead for 80-camera
  rigs. At 6 cameras a decent powered hub is normally fine, but if you see intermittent
  connect failures, try splitting cameras across two hubs/host controllers rather than daisy
  chaining.

## Feeding into FreeMoCap

1. After downloading, each camera's clips live in their own subfolder under the session
   directory.
2. Run FreeMoCap's `skelly_synchronize` (SkellySync in the FreeMoCap v2 UI) against that
   session folder — pick audio cross-correlation (recommended if you clapped) or brightness
   detection (if you used a flash).
3. Import the resulting synchronized, frame-trimmed videos into FreeMoCap as usual for
   calibration and 3D reconstruction.

## Project layout

```
gopro_studio/
  rig.py         # Camera + Rig: connect, settings, synced start/stop, download
  discover.py    # mDNS scan for connected cameras
  config.py      # JSON config (serials, labels, output dir, resolution/fps)
  app.py         # Textual TUI
  setup_cli.py   # gopro-studio-setup CLI for managing config.json
```

## Extending

- `Rig.apply_settings_all` / `Camera.apply_video_settings` are the place to push more than
  resolution/fps (shutter, ISO, lens/FOV, white balance) — see `HttpSettings` in the
  `open-gopro` package for the full list of settings exposed over USB.
- The synced-start barrier lives in `Rig.synced_start` / `Camera._arm_and_fire` — if you want a
  physical trigger (footswitch, MIDI pad) instead of a keybinding, hook it to call
  `app.run_start_recording()`.
